<p>The OPT-2.7B result looks counterintuitive — why is a larger draft model showing lower TPOT than OPT-125M? The answer is that on a modern A100 with batch=1, 125M and 350M models are memory-bandwidth bound and both run nearly as fast as 2.7B, but 2.7B's higher α means fewer target reruns, winning on total latency. For the Qwen family, Qwen2.5-1.5B has the best balance: small enough to be fast, large enough to achieve α ≈ 0.63 — exactly what the N* formula predicts.</p>

<h3>Practical Recipe</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Estimate your target model's perplexity from pre-training scaling laws: <code>ppl = exp(1.817 + 482/N<sup>0.348</sup> + 2085/D<sup>0.366</sup>)</code></li>
  <li>Predict draft perplexity at N* = 2.71×10⁻³ · M + 87M</li>
  <li>Verify α ≥ 0.65 using the affine law: α = −0.0067·x + 0.013·y + 0.642</li>
  <li>If α is too low, train the draft on more data (not larger model — extra data is cheaper)</li>
</ul>
</section>

